course: dictionary
lesson: 1418
title: Caretaker: the person who looks after things
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A caretaker is a person whose job is to look after a building, such as a school. They keep it clean, safe and in good repair. When you meet a new word, read the words around it, because they are its context. «You greet every worker, like a caretaker or cleaner, as kindly as the head teacher.» The words «like a cleaner» and «every worker» show that a caretaker is a kind of worker who helps run the building.
explain: «Caretakers, cleaners and dinner ladies deserve the same respect as the head teacher.» These words are grouped together, so the caretaker is one of the adults who work in the school. The text does not say everything they do, but you can tell they matter as much as the head teacher does.
explain: Caretaker can also mean someone who looks after a person or thing for a while. «Christians believe everything is a gift from God and we are caretakers of it.» Here we look after something we do not own forever. A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence: «we are people who look after it» still makes sense.
fact: A caretaker is a person whose job is to look after a building, such as a school.
fact: A school caretaker keeps the building clean, safe and repaired.
fact: «Caretaker» can also mean someone who looks after someone or something for a while, such as a caretaker manager.
fact: In the USA, a school caretaker is usually called a janitor or custodian.
fact: Pupils should be polite to the caretaker and say thank you, just as they would to the head teacher.
exercise: «Caretakers, cleaners and dinner ladies deserve the same respect as the head teacher.» What is a caretaker's main job? | *To look after a building, keeping it clean, safe and repaired | To cook the school dinners | To teach the maths lessons | To drive the school bus
exercise: In the USA, a school caretaker is usually called what? | A first aider | *A janitor or custodian | A teaching assistant | A head teacher
exercise: «Christians believe everything is a gift from God and we are caretakers of it.» What does caretakers of it mean here? | People who break it | People who sell it | People who own it with no duty to care for it | *People who look after it
test: «You greet every worker, like a caretaker or cleaner, as kindly as the head teacher.» How should pupils treat the caretaker? | Rudely, because they are only staff | By ignoring them | *Kindly, as they would the head teacher | By copying what they do
test: Which person looks after the school building and keeps it safe and repaired? | *The caretaker | The office staff | The nurse | A teaching assistant
exam: Which sentence uses caretaker in its second meaning, looking after something for a while? | The caretaker cleared the snow from the path | *A manager was the caretaker of the shop for a few weeks | The caretaker unlocked the doors at seven o'clock | The caretaker fixed the broken window
